lesson physics-turning-forces v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=6 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Turning forces"
  goal "Work out a force's moment about a pivot, and balance a lever by making the moments on each side equal."

  look {
    scene 28x14 {
      seesaw plank left=6 atleft=2 right=4 atright=3 unit=N marks=true held=0 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      A force that turns something about a pivot has a turning effect called its moment: the force
      times its distance from the pivot. Here the steps are 10 cm apart, so the 6 N weight's moment
      is 6 N times 20 cm, 120 N cm, and the 4 N weight's is 4 N times 30 cm, also 120 N cm. Equal
      moments on each side balance, and the pivot pushes up as hard as both weights pull down, so
      the plank neither turns nor falls. Tools are levers with the pivot in different places: in the
      middle in scissors, at one end in a bottle opener or tweezers. That is why a long spanner, a
      crowbar or the mill's sluice lever lets a small push do a big job.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked physics.moment-of w=4 s=3 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.moment-of count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      practice physics.moment-tips count=2 seed=2
    }
    level medium hard {
      practice physics.moment-pivot-push count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=3
      practice physics.moment-tools count=1 seed=1 hard-seed=2
    }
    practice physics.moment-distance count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.moment-sluice count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.moment-lifts count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.moment-spanner count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
  }
  story {
    show physics.moment-wheelbarrow v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.moment-door
  }
  try stars=3 {
    level easy {
      show physics.moment-all-ways v=0
    }
    level medium {
      show physics.moment-add-weight v=1
    }
    level hard {
      show physics.moment-add-weight v=3
    }
  }
  remember {
    say "A moment is the force times its distance from the pivot, and equal moments balance."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea to watch for is "the heavier side goes down". Children who hold it will get the turning
    questions wrong exactly when a light weight sits far out, which is why those questions are
    there. Ask for both moments aloud, force times distance, before the child says which way it
    turns, and check the units: newtons times centimetres. If it stalls, go back to a ruler balanced
    on a pencil with coins stacked on it, two coins at 5 cm against one at 10 cm, then return to the
    page. Grade four's see-saw lesson already had the balancing rule, so this lesson adds what it
    lacked: the pivot's own push, which balances the weights just as the moments balance each other,
    and where the pivot, the load and the push sit in everyday tools. Off the page, try opening a
    door by pushing near the hinges and then at the handle, and find the pivot in a pair of
    scissors, a bottle opener and tweezers. The three-star question is solved by working out both
    moments first: the extra weight goes on the side with the smaller moment, which is not always
    the side with the lighter weight.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds one moment, the weights and steps are small, the see-saw questions from grade four stay, with the plank tipping clearly one way, the pivot sits close to the gate, the crowbar clearly lifts or not, and the three-star question counts the ways to balance a moment of 12 steps times newtons."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the weights sit further out, the new weight hangs far out, the balanced planks carry bigger weights for the pivot's push, the tool is a pair of tweezers, the crowbar's moments are close, the spanners are 15 and 25 cm, and in the three-star question the heavier weight has the smaller moment."
  }
}
